# Playwright: Record Tests, Codegen & Debugging

## 1. Record Tests Using the Playwright VS Code Extension

The Playwright Test for VS Code extension helps us create, run, and debug Playwright tests directly inside Visual Studio Code.

Steps:

1. Open VS Code.
2. Go to Extensions (`Ctrl + Shift + X`).
3. Search for Playwright Test for VS Code and install it.
4. Open the Command Palette (`Ctrl + Shift + P`).
5. Select Test: Record new.
6. Choose a browser and interact with the application.
7. Playwright generates test code based on your actions.

Example: If you open a login page, enter your email and password, and click Sign In, Playwright can generate the corresponding locator and action code.

## 2. Playwright Codegen

Codegen is a Playwright tool that records browser interactions and generates automation code automatically.

Run this command in the terminal:

```
npx playwright codegen https://example.com
```

A browser window and the Playwright Inspector will open. Perform actions in the browser, and Playwright will generate code based on those interactions.

Example generated code:

```
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('verify page title', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://example.com');

  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Example Domain/);
});
```

Real-time use case: Codegen is useful when starting automation for a new application or identifying suitable locators for web elements.

Note: Always review and improve the generated code before using it in your framework.

## 3. Debug Tests Using `page.pause()`

`page.pause()` pauses test execution at a specific point so that we can inspect the browser, check locators, and debug the test.

Example:

```
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('login test', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://example.com');

  await page.pause();

  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Example Domain/);
});
```

Run the test in debug mode:

```
npx playwright test --debug
```

The Playwright Inspector opens, allowing you to step through the test and inspect elements.

Important: Remove `page.pause()` before running tests in CI/CD pipelines.

## 4. Difference Between the Three

| Feature           | Purpose                                            |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| VS Code Extension | Create, run, and debug tests within VS Code        |
| Codegen           | Generate automation code from browser interactions |
| `page.pause()`    | Pause test execution to inspect and debug          |

## 5. Interview Answer

Question: How do you use Codegen and `page.pause()` in Playwright?

Answer:

“I use Playwright Codegen to record browser interactions and generate automation code with locators and actions. I review the generated code and add appropriate assertions. When debugging a test, I use `page.pause()` to stop execution and inspect elements through the Playwright Inspector. These tools help me develop and debug tests more efficiently.”